"""Teaching replacements within the standard 44-content-slide deck."""
def revise(S,R,C,D):
    refs={
      'g92':('Gisiner and Schusterman (1992)','10.1037/0735-7036.106.1.78','Gisiner R, Schusterman RJ (1992). Sequence, syntax, and semantics: Responses of a language-trained sea lion (Zalophus californianus) to novel sign combinations. Journal of Comparative Psychology 106(1):78–91.'),
      'k94':('Kastak and Schusterman (1994)','10.3758/BF03209162','Kastak D, Schusterman RJ (1994). Transfer of visual identity matching-to-sample in two California sea lions (Zalophus californianus). Animal Learning & Behavior 22(4):427–435.'),
      'k01':('Reichmuth Kastak et al. (2001)','10.1901/jeab.2001.76-131','Reichmuth Kastak C, Schusterman RJ, Kastak D (2001). Equivalence classification by California sea lions using class-specific reinforcers. Journal of the Experimental Analysis of Behavior 76(2):131–158.')}
    for key,(cite,doi,ref) in refs.items(): C[key]=cite;D[key]=doi;R[key]=ref+' https://doi.org/'+doi
    def put(n,title,key,img,panel,cap,body):
        paragraphs=body.split('|')
        S[n-2]={'layout':'figure-right','title':title,'body':paragraphs,'figure':{'path':'figures/'+img+'.png','kind':'article','caption':C[key]+', Fig. '+panel+'. '+cap,'source_url':'https://doi.org/'+D[key]},'cite':C[key],'refs':[R[key]],'transcript':paragraphs}
    put(28,'Sign order controls object-directed actions','g92','Gisiner1992_f1','1A–I','Rocky follows object, modifier, and action signs.',
      'Rocky, a California sea lion, learned hand signs that specified objects, their attributes, and actions. A standard instruction could designate a destination, pause, identify an object by modifiers, and request transport. **Conditional discrimination** means that the appropriate response depends on the preceding cues as well as the currently available objects.|Gisiner and Schusterman recorded orienting during each sign and the final action after a release cue. During 70 anomalous-order probes, 62 orienting responses followed the previously learned sequential rules. The final responses included 55 refusals to complete the command and 15 partial actions.|A modifier narrowed which object Rocky should select, whereas an action sign specified what to do with it. Reordering familiar signs changed the behavior despite retaining familiar components. The experiment connected learned cue order with visual search, waiting at the station, and object-directed movement.')
    put(29,'Identity matching transfers to unfamiliar shapes','k94','Kastak1994_f1','1','Six configurations used for visual identity matching.',
      '**Matching-to-sample** is a choice task in which an initial sample determines the correct comparison. In identity matching, the correct comparison has the same appearance as the sample. Rocky and Rio viewed a sample for approximately 4 s before the two comparison windows opened; a release cue followed after another 2–4 s.|Kastak and Schusterman trained 15 two-stimulus problems and then tested 15 novel problems. Training required 90% correct choices over two consecutive sessions. Correct comparison positions were balanced between left and right, preventing a fixed side response from solving the task.|One sea lion met all transfer criteria on the second test and the other on the third. Both subsequently applied identity matching to stimuli learned previously under different arbitrary matching relations. The learned relationship between sample and choice could therefore guide action when the particular stimulus combination changed.')
    put(30,'Shared outcomes organize learned stimulus classes','k01','Kastak2001_f1','1','Rio performs simple and conditional visual discriminations.',
      'A **functional stimulus class** contains different stimuli that acquire a shared behavioral role. Reichmuth Kastak and colleagues presented two sea lions with 20 visual patterns divided into two sets of 10. In simple discrimination sessions, one set produced correct choices and the other produced incorrect choices; reversing the assignment changed which set was rewarded.|Sessions contained 40 trials in four blocks of 10, with stimulus positions balanced between left and right. The animals acquired classes through repeated reversals, then transferred the class relationships to matching-to-sample. In that task, the sample determined which comparison belonged to the appropriate learned class.|Correct responses to the different classes produced class-specific fish reinforcers. Subsequent experiments related new patterns to established classes through these outcomes. Learning linked visual cues and expected consequences, allowing a nose-touch choice to depend on prior reinforcement relationships rather than physical resemblance alone.')
    chapter='Cook PF, Reichmuth C, Hanke FD (2021). The Mind of a Sea Lion. In: The Brain of the Marine Mammal. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-59184-7_17. Supplementary recordings 17.1–17.3 accompany this chapter and depict the trained procedures, not necessarily the specific published test trials.'
    for n in [28,29,30]: S[n-2]['refs'].append(chapter)
    return S
